\chapter{LQR: Optimal State Feedback}
\label{ch:lqr}

\begin{quote}
\textit{``The theory of optimal control is the theory of how to do the best you can with what you have.''}
\begin{flushright}
--- Richard Bellman, 1957
\end{flushright}
\end{quote}

%% ============================================================================
%% SECTION 1: HISTORICAL MOTIVATION
%% ============================================================================
\section{Historical Motivation: The Quest for Optimal Control}
\label{sec:lqr_history}

\subsection{The Fundamental Dilemma: Where to Place the Poles?}

In Chapter 15, we established that full state feedback can place the closed-loop poles at \textit{any} desired locations, provided the system is controllable. The control law $u = -Kx$ transforms the open-loop dynamics $\dot{x} = Ax + Bu$ into the closed-loop system $\dot{x} = (A - BK)x$, and we can choose the gain matrix $K$ to position the eigenvalues of $(A - BK)$ wherever we wish in the complex plane.

But this freedom creates a profound problem: \textbf{where should we place the poles?}

Consider a simple double integrator system (representing a mass on a frictionless surface):
\begin{equation}
\dot{x} = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix} x + \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} u
\end{equation}

We want to stabilize this system. Some options for closed-loop pole locations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $s = -1 \pm j0$ (real poles at $-1$): Slow, gentle response
    \item $s = -10 \pm j0$ (real poles at $-10$): Fast, aggressive response
    \item $s = -5 \pm j5$ (complex poles): Faster with some overshoot
    \item $s = -100 \pm j0$ (real poles at $-100$): Extremely fast---but at what cost?
\end{itemize}

Faster poles require larger control gains, which mean larger control signals. If our actuator is a DC motor with a 12V supply, demanding 500V is not merely impractical---it is impossible. Even if we could generate such voltages, the motor would saturate, the system would deviate from our linear model, and performance would degrade dramatically.

Before 1960, control engineers faced this dilemma with only intuition and trial-and-error. An experienced designer might ``know'' that poles at $-5$ were ``about right'' for a particular application, but this knowledge was more art than science.

\subsection{Rudolf Kalman and the Birth of Optimal Control Theory}

The breakthrough came from Rudolf Emil Kalman (1930--2016), a Hungarian-American mathematician and electrical engineer whose work would fundamentally transform control theory. In his landmark 1960 paper, ``Contributions to the Theory of Optimal Control,'' published in the \textit{Boletin de la Sociedad Matem\'{a}tica Mexicana}, Kalman posed a revolutionary question:

\textit{Instead of arbitrarily choosing pole locations, can we define what ``optimal'' means mathematically, and then let the mathematics find the best controller?}

Kalman's insight was to introduce a \textbf{cost function} (also called a performance index or objective function) that quantifies what we mean by ``good'' control. The engineer's intuition about ``fast but not too aggressive'' could now be expressed precisely as a mathematical optimization problem.

The Linear Quadratic Regulator (LQR) problem, as it came to be known, asks:
\begin{quote}
Given a linear system $\dot{x} = Ax + Bu$, find the control law $u(t)$ that minimizes the cost function:
\begin{equation}
J = \int_0^\infty \left( x^T Q x + u^T R u \right) dt
\end{equation}
subject to the system dynamics, where $Q \geq 0$ and $R > 0$ are symmetric weighting matrices.
\end{quote}

The term $x^T Q x$ penalizes deviation from the origin (the desired state), while $u^T R u$ penalizes control effort. By adjusting the relative weights $Q$ and $R$, the engineer specifies the tradeoff between performance and effort. The mathematics then finds the optimal balance.

\subsection{Parallel Developments: Pontryagin and Bellman}

Kalman's work did not emerge in isolation. The 1950s and early 1960s saw a remarkable convergence of ideas about optimal control from both sides of the Iron Curtain.

\subsubsection{Pontryagin's Maximum Principle}

In the Soviet Union, Lev Semyonovich Pontryagin (1908--1988) developed what became known as \textbf{Pontryagin's Maximum Principle} (1956--1962). Despite being blind from age 14, Pontryagin became one of the foremost mathematicians of the twentieth century. His approach to optimal control used the calculus of variations and introduced the concept of costate variables (also called adjoint variables or Lagrange multipliers).

The Maximum Principle provides necessary conditions for optimality:
\begin{equation}
H(x^*, u^*, \lambda) \geq H(x^*, u, \lambda) \quad \forall u \in U
\end{equation}
where $H$ is the Hamiltonian, $\lambda$ is the costate vector, and $U$ is the set of admissible controls.

For the LQR problem, Pontryagin's approach leads to the same solution as Kalman's, but the Maximum Principle applies to a broader class of problems, including those with control constraints.

\subsubsection{Bellman's Dynamic Programming}

In the United States, Richard Bellman (1920--1984) at the RAND Corporation developed \textbf{dynamic programming} in the 1950s. His approach was fundamentally different: rather than using calculus of variations, Bellman's method worked backward from the terminal condition, exploiting the \textbf{principle of optimality}:

\begin{quote}
\textit{An optimal policy has the property that whatever the initial state and initial decision are, the remaining decisions must constitute an optimal policy with regard to the state resulting from the first decision.}
\end{quote}

For continuous-time systems, dynamic programming leads to the \textbf{Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) equation}. For the LQR problem, the HJB equation reduces to the algebraic Riccati equation that we will derive later in this chapter.

\subsection{From Theory to the Moon: LQR in the Apollo Program}

The abstract mathematics of optimal control found its most dramatic application in the Apollo program. The Lunar Module (LM) descent guidance system, developed by MIT's Instrumentation Laboratory under Charles Stark Draper, employed LQR principles to navigate the final approach to the lunar surface.

The problem was formidable: the LM had limited fuel, precise position requirements, and safety constraints. The descent had to minimize fuel consumption (control effort) while achieving accurate touchdown (state performance)---exactly the tradeoff that LQR optimizes.

The guidance computer, with less computational power than a modern calculator, could not solve the Riccati equation in real-time. Instead, engineers pre-computed optimal trajectories and stored them as polynomials. The onboard computer interpolated these solutions to generate guidance commands.

On July 20, 1969, when Neil Armstrong guided Eagle to the Sea of Tranquility, he was following trajectories computed using the same LQR principles we study in this chapter. The mathematics that began as abstract theory in Kalman's 1960 paper had, within a decade, helped humanity reach another world.

\subsection{Why LQR Matters Today}

The elegance of LQR lies in its transformation of a vague design problem into a precise optimization problem:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Systematic design}: No more trial-and-error pole placement
    \item \textbf{Guaranteed stability}: The optimal controller is guaranteed stable (for controllable systems)
    \item \textbf{Tunable tradeoffs}: The weights $Q$ and $R$ provide intuitive tuning knobs
    \item \textbf{Theoretical foundation}: Opens the door to more advanced methods (LQG, $H_\infty$, MPC)
    \item \textbf{Computational tractability}: Requires solving only an algebraic matrix equation
\end{enumerate}

As we shall see, LQR is not merely a historical milestone but remains a cornerstone of modern control practice.


%% ============================================================================
%% SECTION 2: MODERN APPLICATIONS
%% ============================================================================
\section{Modern Applications of LQR}
\label{sec:lqr_applications}

While LQR was developed in the 1960s, its principles remain central to cutting-edge applications across engineering, economics, and beyond.

\subsection{Autonomous Vehicle Trajectory Planning}

Self-driving cars must navigate complex environments while balancing multiple objectives: staying centered in the lane (state performance), maintaining smooth steering (control effort), and passenger comfort (minimizing acceleration). Modern autonomous vehicles often use \textbf{Model Predictive Control (MPC)}, which can be viewed as a finite-horizon, constrained extension of LQR.

The cost function for lateral control might be:
\begin{equation}
J = \int_0^T \left( q_y \cdot y_{\text{error}}^2 + q_\psi \cdot \psi_{\text{error}}^2 + r \cdot \delta^2 \right) dt
\end{equation}
where $y_{\text{error}}$ is the lateral deviation from the lane center, $\psi_{\text{error}}$ is the heading error, and $\delta$ is the steering angle.

\subsection{Quadrotor Attitude Control}

Multirotor aircraft (quadcopters, hexacopters) require sophisticated attitude control to maintain stable flight. The dynamics are nonlinear, but around hover conditions, they can be linearized and controlled using LQR.

A quadrotor has 12 states (position, velocity, orientation, angular velocity) and 4 control inputs (motor thrusts). LQR provides a systematic way to design the 4$\times$12 gain matrix $K$ that would be nearly impossible to tune by hand.

The cost function balances:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Attitude errors (roll, pitch, yaw deviations)
    \item Position errors (hovering accuracy)
    \item Motor commands (energy consumption, motor wear)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Economic Policy Optimization}

LQR principles extend beyond engineering to economics. Central banks and governments face optimization problems remarkably similar to control systems:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{States}: Inflation rate, unemployment, GDP growth
    \item \textbf{Controls}: Interest rates, government spending
    \item \textbf{Objective}: Minimize deviation from target inflation and unemployment while avoiding drastic policy swings
\end{itemize}

The economic cost function might be:
\begin{equation}
J = \sum_{t=0}^\infty \beta^t \left[ (\pi_t - \pi^*)^2 + \alpha (u_t - u^*)^2 + \gamma (\Delta i_t)^2 \right]
\end{equation}
where $\pi$ is inflation, $u$ is unemployment, $i$ is the interest rate, and starred quantities are targets. This is discrete-time LQR with discount factor $\beta$.

\subsection{Humanoid Robot Balance Control}

Maintaining balance for a bipedal robot is a challenging control problem. The system is inherently unstable (like an inverted pendulum) and has many degrees of freedom. LQR provides a principled approach to synthesizing the complex coordination required.

Boston Dynamics' Atlas robot and Honda's ASIMO both use optimal control principles derived from LQR. The cost function penalizes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Center-of-mass deviation from the support polygon
    \item Angular momentum (to prevent tipping)
    \item Joint torques (to limit motor stress)
    \item Joint velocities (for smooth motion)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Power Grid Frequency Regulation}

Modern power grids must maintain frequency at exactly 50 Hz (or 60 Hz in North America) despite fluctuating demand. Generators across the grid must coordinate their output to maintain this balance. LQR-based automatic generation control (AGC) optimizes the tradeoff between:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Frequency deviation (state error)
    \item Rate of change of power output (control effort)
    \item Inter-area power flow (coupling between regions)
\end{itemize}


%% ============================================================================
%% SECTION 3: MATHEMATICAL FOUNDATION
%% ============================================================================
\section{Mathematical Foundation}
\label{sec:lqr_math}

We now develop the complete mathematical theory of LQR, from problem formulation through the solution via the Riccati equation.

\subsection{Problem Formulation}

Consider a linear time-invariant (LTI) system in state-space form:
\begin{equation}
\dot{x}(t) = Ax(t) + Bu(t), \quad x(0) = x_0
\label{eq:lqr_dynamics}
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $x(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the state vector
    \item $u(t) \in \mathbb{R}^m$ is the control input vector
    \item $A \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ is the system matrix
    \item $B \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times m}$ is the input matrix
    \item $x_0$ is the initial state
\end{itemize}

The \textbf{infinite-horizon LQR problem} seeks to find the control input $u(t)$ that minimizes the quadratic cost function:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{J = \int_0^\infty \left( x^T(t) Q x(t) + u^T(t) R u(t) \right) dt}
\label{eq:lqr_cost}
\end{equation}
subject to the dynamics~\eqref{eq:lqr_dynamics}, where:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $Q \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ is a symmetric positive semi-definite matrix ($Q \geq 0$)
    \item $R \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times m}$ is a symmetric positive definite matrix ($R > 0$)
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Interpretation of the Cost Function}

The cost function~\eqref{eq:lqr_cost} has a beautiful geometric interpretation. Consider the scalar case with one state and one input:
\begin{equation}
J = \int_0^\infty \left( q \cdot x^2 + r \cdot u^2 \right) dt
\end{equation}

At each instant, the ``instantaneous cost'' is $q \cdot x^2 + r \cdot u^2$. If we plot this as a function of $x$ and $u$, we get an elliptical paraboloid---a ``bowl'' shape with minimum at the origin. The total cost $J$ is the integral of this instantaneous cost over all time.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
    width=10cm, height=8cm,
    view={45}{30},
    xlabel={State $x$},
    ylabel={Control $u$},
    zlabel={Cost $qx^2 + ru^2$},
    colormap/viridis,
    title={LQR Cost Function: A Quadratic Bowl},
    xmin=-2, xmax=2,
    ymin=-2, ymax=2,
    zmin=0, zmax=4,
]
\addplot3[
    surf,
    domain=-2:2,
    domain y=-2:2,
    samples=30,
    samples y=30,
    opacity=0.8,
] {0.5*x^2 + 0.5*y^2};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The LQR cost function forms a quadratic bowl. The optimal controller drives the system toward the minimum at the origin while balancing state deviation ($x$-axis) against control effort ($u$-axis).}
\label{fig:cost_bowl}
\end{figure}

In the multi-variable case, $x^T Q x$ and $u^T R u$ are quadratic forms. The matrix $Q$ defines the shape of the state penalty ellipsoid, while $R$ defines the control penalty ellipsoid.

\textbf{Why $Q \geq 0$ but $R > 0$?}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $Q \geq 0$ (positive semi-definite): We may not care about all states. For example, if $Q = \text{diag}(1, 0)$, we penalize only the first state.
    \item $R > 0$ (positive definite): We must penalize all control inputs. If $R$ were singular, the optimal control could become unbounded, leading to an ill-posed problem.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Solution via Calculus of Variations}

The LQR problem is a constrained optimization problem: minimize $J$ subject to the differential equation constraint~\eqref{eq:lqr_dynamics}. We solve it using the calculus of variations and Lagrange multipliers.

\subsubsection{The Hamiltonian}

Introduce the costate (adjoint) vector $\lambda(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n$ and form the \textbf{Hamiltonian}:
\begin{equation}
H(x, u, \lambda) = x^T Q x + u^T R u + \lambda^T (Ax + Bu)
\label{eq:hamiltonian}
\end{equation}

The Hamiltonian combines the instantaneous cost with the dynamics constraint, weighted by $\lambda$.

\subsubsection{Necessary Conditions for Optimality}

Pontryagin's Maximum Principle (or equivalently, the Euler-Lagrange equations) provides the necessary conditions for optimality:

\textbf{1. State equation} (from the constraint):
\begin{equation}
\dot{x} = \frac{\partial H}{\partial \lambda} = Ax + Bu
\end{equation}

\textbf{2. Costate equation}:
\begin{equation}
\dot{\lambda} = -\frac{\partial H}{\partial x} = -2Qx - A^T \lambda
\label{eq:costate}
\end{equation}

\textbf{3. Optimality condition} (stationarity with respect to $u$):
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial H}{\partial u} = 0 \implies 2Ru + B^T \lambda = 0
\end{equation}

Solving for the optimal control:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{u^* = -\frac{1}{2} R^{-1} B^T \lambda}
\label{eq:optimal_u_lambda}
\end{equation}

Note that $R^{-1}$ exists because $R > 0$.

\subsubsection{The Riccati Equation}

The costate $\lambda(t)$ is not directly useful---we need $u$ in terms of $x$. We hypothesize a linear relationship:
\begin{equation}
\lambda(t) = 2P(t) x(t)
\label{eq:lambda_Px}
\end{equation}
where $P(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ is a symmetric matrix to be determined.

Substituting~\eqref{eq:lambda_Px} into~\eqref{eq:optimal_u_lambda}:
\begin{equation}
u^* = -R^{-1} B^T P x
\label{eq:optimal_u_K}
\end{equation}

This is state feedback with gain $K = R^{-1} B^T P$.

Now we differentiate $\lambda = 2Px$ and match with the costate equation~\eqref{eq:costate}:
\begin{align}
\dot{\lambda} &= 2\dot{P}x + 2P\dot{x} \\
&= 2\dot{P}x + 2P(Ax + Bu^*) \\
&= 2\dot{P}x + 2P(Ax - BR^{-1}B^T P x) \\
&= 2\left( \dot{P} + PA - PBR^{-1}B^T P \right) x
\end{align}

From the costate equation~\eqref{eq:costate}:
\begin{equation}
\dot{\lambda} = -2Qx - A^T (2Px) = -2(Q + A^T P)x
\end{equation}

Equating the two expressions for $\dot{\lambda}$:
\begin{equation}
\dot{P} + PA - PBR^{-1}B^T P = -(Q + A^T P)
\end{equation}

Rearranging yields the \textbf{Differential Riccati Equation (DRE)}:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{\dot{P} = -PA - A^T P + PBR^{-1}B^T P - Q}
\label{eq:dre}
\end{equation}

\subsection{The Algebraic Riccati Equation}

For the \textit{infinite-horizon} problem, we seek a steady-state solution where $\dot{P} = 0$. Setting the time derivative to zero in~\eqref{eq:dre} yields the \textbf{Algebraic Riccati Equation (ARE)}:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{A^T P + PA - PBR^{-1}B^T P + Q = 0}
\label{eq:are}
\end{equation}

This is a system of $n(n+1)/2$ nonlinear algebraic equations in the entries of the symmetric matrix $P$.

\begin{theorem}[Existence and Uniqueness of the ARE Solution]
\label{thm:are_existence}
If the pair $(A, B)$ is \textbf{stabilizable} and the pair $(A, C)$ is \textbf{detectable}, where $C$ is any matrix such that $Q = C^T C$, then the ARE~\eqref{eq:are} has a unique positive semi-definite solution $P \geq 0$. Furthermore, the closed-loop system $(A - BK)$ is asymptotically stable.
\end{theorem}

For our purposes, if the system is \textbf{controllable}, then stabilizability holds. If $Q > 0$ or if $(A, Q^{1/2})$ is observable, then detectability holds.

\subsection{The Optimal Gain and Closed-Loop System}

Once we solve the ARE for $P$, the optimal feedback gain is:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{K = R^{-1} B^T P}
\label{eq:lqr_gain}
\end{equation}

The optimal control law is:
\begin{equation}
u^*(t) = -Kx(t)
\end{equation}

The closed-loop dynamics become:
\begin{equation}
\dot{x} = (A - BK)x = A_{\text{cl}} x
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Guaranteed Stability]
If $(A, B)$ is controllable and the ARE has a solution $P \geq 0$, then all eigenvalues of $A_{\text{cl}} = A - BK$ have strictly negative real parts. The closed-loop system is asymptotically stable.
\end{theorem}

This is a profound result: \textbf{by minimizing the cost function, stability is automatically guaranteed}. We do not need to check stability separately---it comes ``for free'' from the optimization.

\subsection{The Optimal Cost}

An elegant result relates the optimal cost to the solution $P$. If the system starts at $x_0$, the minimum achievable cost is:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{J^* = x_0^T P x_0}
\label{eq:optimal_cost}
\end{equation}

This provides a measure of ``how far'' the initial state is from equilibrium, weighted by the cost function.

\subsection{Tuning Q and R: The Art Within the Science}

While LQR removes the arbitrary choice of pole locations, it introduces a new choice: the weighting matrices $Q$ and $R$. This is not merely shifting the problem; the $Q$ and $R$ matrices have physical interpretations that make tuning more intuitive.

\subsubsection{Physical Interpretation}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Large $Q$}: Heavily penalizes state deviation. The controller will be aggressive, driving states to zero quickly, even if it requires large control efforts.
    \item \textbf{Large $R$}: Heavily penalizes control effort. The controller will be gentle, using small inputs, even if states take longer to converge.
    \item \textbf{Relative scaling}: Only the ratio $Q/R$ matters. Scaling both by the same constant does not change the optimal controller.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Bryson's Rule}

Arthur Bryson proposed a practical rule for selecting diagonal $Q$ and $R$ matrices:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{Q_{ii} = \frac{1}{(\text{max acceptable } x_i)^2}, \quad R_{jj} = \frac{1}{(\text{max acceptable } u_j)^2}}
\label{eq:bryson}
\end{equation}

\textbf{Example}: For an inverted pendulum:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Maximum acceptable angle: $15°$ ($\approx 0.26$ rad)
    \item Maximum acceptable angular velocity: $50°$/s ($\approx 0.87$ rad/s)
    \item Maximum acceptable voltage: 12 V
\end{itemize}

Using Bryson's rule:
\begin{equation}
Q = \begin{bmatrix} 1/0.26^2 & 0 \\ 0 & 1/0.87^2 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} 14.8 & 0 \\ 0 & 1.32 \end{bmatrix}, \quad R = \frac{1}{12^2} \approx 0.007
\end{equation}

Bryson's rule provides a physically motivated starting point; further tuning may be needed based on simulation and experiment.

\subsubsection{Effect on Closed-Loop Poles}

As $Q/R$ increases (more aggressive control), the closed-loop poles move further into the left half-plane. Figure~\ref{fig:pole_migration} illustrates this pole migration for a double integrator.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
    width=12cm, height=8cm,
    xlabel={Real axis},
    ylabel={Imaginary axis},
    xmin=-12, xmax=2,
    ymin=-6, ymax=6,
    axis lines=middle,
    grid=both,
    title={LQR Pole Locations as $Q/R$ Varies (Double Integrator)},
    legend pos=south west,
]
% Open-loop poles at origin
\addplot[only marks, mark=x, mark size=4pt, thick, black]
    coordinates {(0,0)};
\addlegendentry{Open-loop poles}

% Closed-loop poles for different Q/R ratios
\addplot[only marks, mark=o, mark size=3pt, blue]
    coordinates {(-0.707,0.707) (-0.707,-0.707)};
\addlegendentry{$Q/R = 1$}

\addplot[only marks, mark=square, mark size=3pt, red]
